Prior work records follow chronologically; this section describes 0.16.

Zero Media 0.14 work state - 2026-11-20

New implementation is exclusively original Zero. The new native image
format is .zmedia (ZMEDIA01), with an exact bounded layout and an original
SHA-256 implementation. It contains an independently built whole-disk
payload, not an ISO. BIOS32/64 and UEFI32/64 loaders are provided.

Native Linux x86-64 and Windows x64 programs: zero-media, zero-flash,
zero-drives, zero-disk and zero-media-check. All new sources are in media/.
The source snapshot is validated before a write target is opened. The
native writer restricts targets to identified removable USB whole disks,
requires explicit target confirmation, checks identity again, flushes and
compares every written byte. Obsolete backup-GPT sectors beyond the image
are cleared with bounds checks. It never silently unmounts filesystems.

Zero's flash writer is an original UEFI application launched from the new
media boot menu before the OS kernel. It reads system.zmedia from a
firmware-readable volume, protects its boot/source disks, and writes only
eligible USB destinations after confirmation. This does not implement a
runtime USB driver in the Zero kernel; native in-kernel USB access remains
unfinished. BIOS boots the system but does not run the UEFI flash utility.

compiler.zero now supports existing call7 syntax for x64 raw/UEFI targets.
Its IA32 ABI and other target restrictions are unchanged. The native own
builder has70stages and produces66artifacts, including both host editions,
both firmware editions, media menus and disk-building components.
media/README.ztext gives the exact workflow and platform restrictions.
media/VERIFICATION.ztext records the final artifact checks. Tests use
private regular files and emulated USB devices; physical flash drives and
native Windows hardware are not certified. User data disks are preserved.

Previous baseline and retained capabilities follow (historical0.13 record):

Zero OS 0.13 work state - 2026-11-18

All new implementation is original Zero. Ezero remains the simpler language
implemented on Zero, and the editors can edit and compile both languages.
No Chromium source, foreign GUI toolkit, C, assembly or Python implementation
was added. External firmware, emulators, host OS APIs and private validation
tools are not project implementation. Human conversation is Russian; system
messages are English. Visible indexes and line numbers begin at one.

New complete components: bounded HTTP HTML/plain-text browser, original
ZBROW001 page documents, navigation/history/relative URLs/redirects, guest
Zero Studio editor and native Linux/Windows Zero Studio, actual PS/2 pointer.
The browser is a text-oriented first version, not a full modern-web engine.
HTTPS, CSS layout, JavaScript/DOM, forms and browser media remain absent.
Native editor holds one ASCII document up to65535bytes and builds both
languages using the original compiler apps. Guest editor holds400bytes,
uses actual filesystem operations, and builds/runs both languages in the OS.

Source regeneration is original Zero: tools/integrate.zero composes kernel,
Ezero, picture and browser sources; editor/host/compose.zero composes Studio.
tools/build.zero produces49artifacts, requiring an own compiler fixed point.
Bootstrap remains the earlier Zero-built bootstrap/zero64.zapp application.
Compiler source limit524288, token limit240000, output limit524288,64locals.
IA32 short literals and local addresses preserve full64-bit arithmetic.

ISO remains18890752bytes; kernel64slot475136, kernel32slot507904.
Current kernels418178/506839bytes; IA32 has1065bytes spare. Do not grow the
BIOS slot into EBDA/VGA memory. Bundle has8apps in1MiB:6compiler pipeline apps
plus readers at339968/819200. Packer slots16384, reader slots143360;
runtimeprefix962560/body966656 remain unchanged.
